---
/**
 * REWRITTEN TO SS_WRITING_FOR_KASPAR.md, 26 Nov 2026 (a founder: the remaining
 * area pages rewritten "based on the new knowledge" of what Kaspar comments on;
 * ops rulings D3 and D7). Gone: the tavern, the name, the appraisal lines, the
 * listing count, Article 4 and every source named in a sentence (D7), and
 * "bespoke carpentry" (D3). Now: a two-sentence opening; six sections, each
 * headed with the fitting, the room and the place (mansion flats, large
 * upper-floor windows, loft rooms, alcoves, listed homes on K18, access in
 * mansion blocks on K19); FAQ answers in "we" and "you" from his own text. The
 * chalet drawing is off (its caption narrated the 1804 tavern); studio-mansion
 * and gabled-semis stay beside the sections they show; the other four sections
 * are plain text, as every other drawing belongs to another area page. The
 * price cards and the price section's words are unchanged. No Ask brackets:
 * every figure on the page is his.
 * FACT LIST (claim: source).
 *   - Mansion-block flats on Canfield Gardens and east of Fairhazel Gardens:
 *     facts file, S1 5.5.
 *   - Very large upper-floor windows on Compayne Gardens: facts file, S1 5.12.
 *   - Large houses in South Hampstead with loft rooms: S1 5.8 ("often again
 *     with attics"); large late Victorian houses: S1 3.1.
 *   - Belsize Road: S1 5.6 (terraces with basements); no feature claimed, the
 *     sentence is "if your home ... has a chimney breast".
 *   - Maresfield Gardens and Netherhall Gardens chosen for the listed step:
 *     S2 draft 1.3 ("numerous listed buildings"), against one listed building
 *     in South Hampstead (S1 6.3). Used to pick the streets only.
 *   - Camden's listed buildings page links a map of listed buildings; its
 *     controlled parking zones page searches by street or postcode; trade
 *     permits are for people working for a resident: all three pages read
 *     26 Nov 2026 (200).
 *   - Wardrobes wall to wall; door style and finish the customer's choice:
 *     facts table, 23 Nov Camden. Shaker or handleless doors in mansion flats:
 *     his Hampstead text (8 and 10 Nov, live).
 *   - A wardrobe run to the ceiling: facts table, 26 Nov Islington.
 *   - Storage on a side wall keeps the window clear: facts table, 26 Nov
 *     Islington (lower-ground row). The low space beneath a window put to use:
 *     his Islington opening, 26 Nov.
 *   - Hanging where the ceiling is highest, low drawers or cupboards under the
 *     slope: facts table, 23 Nov Camden and 26 Nov Islington. Every door
 *     checked beneath the slope: his built-in wardrobe guide (24 Oct),
 *     "Wardrobes under a sloping ceiling".
 *   - Alcove units as low cupboards under open shelves or full-height
 *     cupboards: his Islington FAQ, 26 Nov. Shelf openings of around 250 to
 *     350 mm for books, more for larger pieces: facts table, his alcove guide
 *     (14 Oct).
 *   - Ask the council before work starts, especially where skirting or
 *     panelling changes: facts table, 26 Nov Islington. A wardrobe finished
 *     below the cornice, the moulding left visible: facts table, 10 Nov
 *     Hampstead and 23 Nov Camden.
 *   - Where to unload and park and how materials come in, agreed before
 *     fitting day; who arranges a permit, agreed: facts table, 8 and 10 Nov
 *     Hampstead, 23 Nov Camden, 26 Nov Islington.
 *   - Lift and building access details asked of the customer; insurance and
 *     safety documents to the managing agent: facts table, 8 Nov Hampstead.
 *   - Furniture in sections, assembled in the room, where access is tight:
 *     facts table, 10 Nov Hampstead.
 *   - Normally 8am to 5pm on weekdays, adjusted to the building: facts table,
 *     8 Nov Hampstead.
 *   - Floors and stairs protected along the delivery route: facts table,
 *     23 Nov Camden.
 *   - FAQs (questions kept): drawers behind the doors or as their own section
 *     beneath them, hinged doors planned around the drawers (his wardrobe
 *     guide; facts table, 23 Nov Camden); hinged against sliding doors (his
 *     wardrobe guide, "Choose hinged or sliding doors"); double hanging and a
 *     full-length section sized from the longest garment on its hanger (his
 *     wardrobe guide, "Storage").
 * The older notes below describe the 17 Nov draft. Most of its text is gone.
 *
 * Swiss Cottage, 17 November 2026. A draft for Kaspar to read on the review
 * link (F2F ops clients/structure-and-style/areas-review); held noindex by
 * areaSwissCottageCheck until he has read it and his changes are in. Nothing
 * goes live before he has approved every area page (Denis, 15 Nov 2026).
 *
 * ON THE PRIMROSE HILL MODEL from the start (a founder's go, 17 Nov; Denis,
 * 15 Nov: "use the same design and structure for the next articles"; ops
 * README, "The structure rule, applied"; ops SS_AREA_PAGE_RULES_v1.0.md, a
 * draft, read for its plan items): address /areas/swiss-cottage/ with 301s
 * from /carpenter-swiss-cottage/ (netlify.toml and the Worker's list); the
 * title, heading, kicker and description lead with bespoke wardrobes, the
 * area's largest product search on record (the reasons are in areas.ts); the
 * opening carries "bespoke carpentry" and "bespoke joinery" once each; a line
 * drawing beside each section that describes something (src/data/
 * area-drawings.ts: a chalet in the style of the 1804 tavern, a mansion block
 * with big studio windows on its top floor, a pair of gabled semis with attic
 * rooms), none where a section is rules or access only; the guide prices as
 * cards; register photos and one labelled AI image (ssp-032 and ssp-033,
 * cropped to 3:2, and AI grey sliding-door wardrobe, Figma node 35:2).
 *
 * Sources. Every local fact from the ops facts file
 * research/area-pages-2026-11-14/facts-swiss-cottage-2026-11-14.md, read
 * 14 Nov 2026:
 *   Camden's South Hampstead Conservation Area Character Appraisal and
 *     Management Strategy (February 2011, "S1"): Swiss Cottage proper on the
 *     east side of Finchley Road, around the library and the former Swiss
 *     Tavern, built 1804 in the style of a Swiss chalet on the site of a
 *     tollgate keeper's cottage (2.7); the conservation area called Swiss
 *     Cottage until February 2011 (1.2); east of Fairhazel Gardens, terraces
 *     forming imposing mansion blocks of 3 and 4 storeys, rising to 5 on
 *     Canfield Gardens (5.5); many houses east of Fairhazel Gardens and on
 *     Compayne Gardens with very large upper-floor windows suggesting studio
 *     design (5.12); the central part large semi-detached houses of 3 and 4
 *     storeys, red or gault brick, turrets, gables and tall chimneys (3.1,
 *     5.7; 3.1 calls them late-Victorian); the west part detached, often with attics, some with raised
 *     basements (5.8); one listed building, the Grade II Church of St James,
 *     Sherriff Road (6.3).
 *   Camden's Belsize Conservation Area Statement (2002, "S3"): Eton Avenue,
 *     Fellows Road and Lancaster Gardens added in 1985 to protect groups of
 *     large detached houses and some terraces.
 *   Camden's Fitzjohns/Netherhall appraisal, DRAFT of December 2021 ("S2";
 *     the adopted December 2022 version could not be read): the area
 *     "contains numerous listed buildings", among them the Freud Museum,
 *     20 Maresfield Gardens (Grade II).
 *   Camden's Article 4 page ("S5"): directions adopted 9 June 2026 for South
 *     Hampstead, Belsize and Fitzjohns/Netherhall, removing some permitted
 *     development rights; its June 2026 documents could not be read.
 *   Camden's listed buildings, parking zone and trade permit pages ("S6",
 *     "S7"): a trade permit is valid in resident bays and on yellow lines in
 *     the zone, with no guaranteed space.
 * Left out: the Character Study's mansion-block description (not read in
 * full), King's Gardens and West End Court (West Hampstead's), Swiss Cottage
 * Library (a landmark, not a home), Finchley Road station.
 *
 * WHOSE WORDS. From what he approved: the wardrobe guide's lines on sloping
 * ceilings ("Use the taller part of the room for hanging clothes and the
 * lower areas for shelves, drawers or cupboards. The position of the wardrobe
 * front matters: a slope can reduce usable depth as well as height"), on
 * drawers, on hinged and sliding doors, and on hanging space and shelves
 * (the questions); the guarantee; the fitted wardrobe, walk-in and alcove
 * price lines as approved on Hampstead. Two conduct lines: parking,
 * deliveries and access agreed before installation, as on Camden, Belsize
 * Park and Kentish Town, and the protocol's approved conduct fact 5 on the
 * managing agent (section 11), in its publishable form (Maida Vale's old
 * draft carries it too: leave it off that page when it is rebuilt).
 * Everything else is new copy for him to check.
 *
 * CHECKED 17 Nov by three independent reviewers (copy rules, facts against
 * the facts file and approved text, the swap test against the six other area
 * pages). Changed on their findings: the opening's "whether ... or ..." (a
 * banned shape) gone; "west of the junction" (no source) replaced by S1's
 * Canfield Gardens line; "none of its houses is listed" (a deduction) now
 * "most homes there are not listed"; "all three areas" named; "some
 * terraces" restored; the slope lines back to his own imperative; mansion
 * blocks, conservation areas, gault and double hanging explained once; the
 * Camden listed-building and parking lines (near-copies of other pages)
 * replaced by this page's own; one wording for mansion blocks throughout;
 * captions say the drawings show a style; alts say oak-effect throughout.
 * Kept on purpose: "Parking, deliveries and access are agreed with you
 * before installation." (as Camden, Belsize Park and Kentish Town; the
 * protocol keeps keys and access per job, which a founder may want to
 * weigh), the guarantee sentence and the price card lines.
 *
 * Street names are Camden's documents', describing the houses. None is a
 * place we claim to have worked. No review block: no review names Swiss
 * Cottage.
 *
 * FAQ sources, all three new, none on another area page: "Can you put a
 * chest of drawers inside a wardrobe?" and "What storage suits the inside of
 * a wardrobe?" (Google People also ask, wardrobe drawers inside: "What is the
 * best storage option for inside my wardrobe?", "best" taken out),
 * "Which is better for a wardrobe, sliding or hinged doors?" (People also ask
 * on sliding or hinged wardrobe doors: "Which is better for a wardrobe,
 * sliding or door?", made whole); UK, English, desktop, 17 Nov 2026. The
 * boxes for bespoke wardrobes swiss cottage and fitted wardrobes swiss
 * cottage held costs, a design-trends question, a "70/30 rule" with no fixed
 * meaning, and company names. Answers from the wardrobe guide he approved
 * (drawers behind the doors or as their own section; hinged doors where they
 * can swing, sliding where space is limited; double hanging, long garments,
 * shelves).
 */
import AreaPage from "../../components/AreaPage.astro";
import Wrap from "../../components/ds/Wrap.astro";
import SectionHead from "../../components/ds/SectionHead.astro";
import Faq from "../../components/ds/Faq.astro";
import Link from "../../components/ds/Link.astro";
import AreaSide from "../../components/AreaSide.astro";
import PriceCards from "../../components/PriceCards.astro";

import { AREAS } from "../../data/areas";

const area = AREAS.find((a) => a.slug === "areas/swiss-cottage")!;

const PRICES = [
  {
    art: "fitted-wardrobes",
    name: "Fitted wardrobes",
    href: "/fitted-wardrobes/",
    price: "From £2,300 for a 2-door run up to 1.2m in melamine, and from £4,000 at 4 doors.",
    moves: "Width, finish, internal drawers, integrated lighting and more complex designs move the figure.",
  },
  {
    art: "walk-in-wardrobes",
    name: "Walk-in wardrobes",
    href: "/walk-in-wardrobes/",
    price: "Typically from around £5,000.",
    moves: "The size of the room, number of fitted runs, internal layout, materials and finishes, and integrated lighting move the figure.",
  },
  {
    art: "alcove-units",
    name: "Alcove units",
    href: "/alcove-units/",
    price: "From £3,300 for a pair in melamine, and from £6,000 in oak veneer.",
    moves: "Finish, shelving detail, drawers and lighting move the figure. It assumes base cupboards with shelving over, up to about 1m per alcove.",
  },
];
const P2 = "margin-top:var(--space-10)";
const TEXT = "max-width:var(--measure)";
const SECTION = "padding-bottom:var(--section-y)";
const EXT = { target: "_blank", rel: "noopener" };
---

<AreaPage area={area}>
  <Fragment slot="lede">
    <p>
      We design and fit <Link href="/fitted-wardrobes/">bespoke wardrobes</Link>, <Link href="/walk-in-wardrobes/">walk-in wardrobes</Link> and <Link href="/alcove-units/">alcove units</Link> for homes around Swiss Cottage, including South Hampstead and the streets off Finchley Road. In mansion flats and large Victorian houses, we can plan the storage around a sloping loft ceiling or a very large upper-floor window.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Mansion flats" level={4}>Bespoke wardrobes for mansion flats in Swiss Cottage</SectionHead>
    <div style={TEXT}>
      <p>
        On Canfield Gardens and the streets east of Fairhazel Gardens, many homes are flats in mansion blocks. If you live in one, we can fit a bedroom wardrobe wall to wall, with Shaker or handleless doors in the finish you choose. We can run it to the ceiling, so spare bedding and suitcases can go up high, out of the way.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Large windows" level={4}>Fitted storage around large upper-floor windows in Swiss Cottage</SectionHead>
    <AreaSide drawing="studio-mansion">
      <p>
        Some homes on Compayne Gardens have very large windows on their upper floors. If your room has one, we can fit a full-height wardrobe along a side wall and keep the window clear. Below the window, low drawers or a cupboard can use the space without covering the glass.
      </p>
    </AreaSide>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Loft rooms" level={4}>Fitted wardrobes for loft rooms in South Hampstead</SectionHead>
    <AreaSide drawing="gabled-semis">
      <p>
        Many of the larger houses in South Hampstead have loft rooms with sloping ceilings. Coats and dresses can hang where the room is tallest, with low drawers or cupboards fitted under the slope. We'll plan every door so it opens fully beneath the sloping ceiling.
      </p>
    </AreaSide>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Alcoves" level={4}>Alcove units for living rooms in Swiss Cottage's Victorian houses</SectionHead>
    <div style={TEXT}>
      <p>
        If your home on Belsize Road or nearby has a chimney breast, we can fit <Link href="/alcove-units/">alcove units</Link> on both sides. Each side can have low cupboards under open shelves, or a full-height cupboard with doors to the top. We'll <Link href="/journal/alcove-units-guide/">space the shelves</Link> for what you keep on them, with openings of around 250 to 350 mm for books and more for larger pieces.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Listed homes" level={4}>Fitted joinery in listed homes around Swiss Cottage</SectionHead>
    <div style={TEXT}>
      <p>
        For a home on Maresfield Gardens, Netherhall Gardens or nearby, you can see if it's listed on <Link href="https://www.camden.gov.uk/listed-buildings" {...EXT}>Camden Council's listed buildings page</Link>. If it is, ask the council before work starts whether the joinery you're planning needs listed building consent. This matters most if fitting it means removing or changing original skirting or wall panelling.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        Where a room keeps its original cornice, we can finish a wardrobe just below it, so the moulding stays in view.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Access" level={4}>Lifts, managing agents and parking on fitting days in Swiss Cottage</SectionHead>
    <div style={TEXT}>
      <p>
        Before fitting day, we'll agree with you where to unload and park, and how the furniture comes into your home. You can find your street's parking zone on <Link href="https://www.camden.gov.uk/controlled-parking-zones" {...EXT}>Camden Council's controlled parking zones page</Link>. If we need a <Link href="https://www.camden.gov.uk/trade-permits" {...EXT}>trade permit</Link>, we'll agree with you who applies for it.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        In a mansion block, we may ask you for details of the lift and building access. We can send our insurance and safety documents to the managing agent before fitting day. If the lift or stairs are tight, we can plan the furniture in sections and assemble it in the room.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        We normally work 8am to 5pm on weekdays, and we can keep to different hours if your building sets them. Before any materials come in on fitting day, we protect the floors and stairs along the delivery route.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Guide prices" level={4}>Guide prices for wardrobes, walk-ins and alcove units</SectionHead>
    <AreaSide>
      <p>Every figure here is a starting price, and a larger or more detailed piece costs more.</p>
      <p style={P2}>
        The guarantee covers installation and workmanship for 2 years and the structure of the joinery for 10 years. Hardware is on the manufacturer's warranty, and doors and drawers are adjusted in the first 2 years. Our <Link href="/fitted-wardrobe-cost/">cost guide</Link> shows what moves the price of a fitted wardrobe.
      </p>
      <PriceCards slot="side" items={PRICES} />
    </AreaSide>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Questions" scribe>Asked before enquiring</SectionHead>
    <div style="margin-top:var(--space-12);max-width:var(--measure);border-bottom:var(--rule-hair)">
      <Faq q="Can you put a chest of drawers inside a wardrobe?">
        We can fit drawers inside the wardrobe, behind its doors, so you won't need a separate chest. They can also sit in their own section beneath the doors, which lets you open a drawer without opening a door first. With hinged doors, we size the doors and place the hinges so every drawer has room to open.
      </Faq>
      <Faq q="Which is better for a wardrobe, sliding or hinged doors?">
        It depends on the space in front of the wardrobe. Hinged doors suit many layouts and come in a wide choice of styles, as long as there's room for them to swing open. Sliding doors need no swing space, but part of the wardrobe stays covered while another part is open.
      </Faq>
      <Faq q="What storage suits the inside of a wardrobe?">
        We plan the inside around the clothes you own. Double hanging, with two rails one above the other, suits shirts, jackets and folded trousers. Long dresses and coats need a full-length section, sized from your longest one on its hanger so the hem hangs clear.
      </Faq>
    </div>
  </Wrap>
</AreaPage>
